      "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{18y^3}$ given that $y \\geq 0.$",
      "worked_solution": "We first write $18y^3$ as the product $9y^2\\cdot 2y$ which contains a perfect square $(9y^2).$ Then, we separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{18y^3} &= \\\n\t\t\\sqrt{9y^2 \\cdot 2y} &= \\\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\\sqrt{9y^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\t\t\n\t\t\\sqrt{(3y)^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\t\t\n\t\t|3y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\t\t\n\t\t|3| \\cdot |y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\n\t\t3 \\cdot |y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y}\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince $y \\geq 0,$ we have $|y|=y,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t3 \\cdot |y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\n\t\t3 \\cdot y \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\n\t\t3y\\sqrt{2y}. &\n\t}\n$$",
